- It can wait, Katie, please.

532
00:31:17.913 --> 00:31:22.782
- Get me out, I wanna
go home, I wanna go home.

533
00:31:23.881 --> 00:31:25.981
- Mr. Fenton, are you okay?

534
00:31:25.982 --> 00:31:27.214
You alright?

535
00:31:27.215 --> 00:31:29.782
Your family just left, you're okay.

536
00:31:29.783 --> 00:31:31.181
Just take a deep breath, it's fine.

537
00:31:31.182 --> 00:31:33.283
I'm Nurse Katie, remember?
- Where's my son?

538
00:31:34.150 --> 00:31:35.982
- I know, it's just the new
meds, you'll get used to 'em,

539
00:31:35.983 --> 00:31:39.216
don't worry, you're
alright, you're alright.

540
00:31:39.217 --> 00:31:41.051
You're alright.

541
00:32:04.722 --> 00:32:06.722
- Hi.

542
00:32:07.889 --> 00:32:09.288
I'm Michael.

543
00:32:09.289 --> 00:32:11.922
I'm from down the hall.

544
00:32:13.724 --> 00:32:15.222
- Michael.

545
00:32:15.223 --> 00:32:17.989
- Would it be alright if I
read to you for a little bit?

546
00:32:17.990 --> 00:32:21.257
This book always seems
to make me feel better.

547
00:32:21.258 --> 00:32:22.658
- Take it.

548
00:32:23.658 --> 00:32:25.190
- See if I can find something.

549
00:32:32.727 --> 00:32:35.392
"Look at the birds of the air,

550
00:32:35.393 --> 00:32:40.394
"they do not sow or reap
or store away in barns,

551
00:32:40.561 --> 00:32:43.226
"and yet your Heavenly Father feeds them.

552
00:32:43.227 --> 00:32:46.262
"Are you not much more
valuable than they?"

553
00:32:46.295 --> 00:32:48.662
- Don't.
- Who...

554
00:32:50.029 --> 00:32:52.430
- Don't you read to me that garbage.

555
00:32:59.164 --> 00:33:00.296
I'm gonna do you a favor

556
00:33:00.297 --> 00:33:03.364
and I'm gonna tell it
to you straight, okay?

557
00:33:03.365 --> 00:33:04.764
You've probably carried that Bible around

558
00:33:04.765 --> 00:33:08.432
and you just reads about
anyone that'll listen to ya.

559
00:33:08.433 --> 00:33:11.898
Probably even think your
Savin' souls, don't you?

560
00:33:11.899 --> 00:33:13.432
And maybe you think
that God put you in here

561
00:33:13.433 --> 00:33:18.300
so he'd give you a great
opportunity to reach others.

562
00:33:19.601 --> 00:33:22.467
But here's the one cruel irony,

563
00:33:22.468 --> 00:33:24.233
that unfortunately, you can't see with that

564
00:33:24.234 --> 00:33:27.569
young, naive mind of yours.

565
00:33:28.602 --> 00:33:32.202
The one who needs saving is you.

566
00:33:33.670 --> 00:33:35.536
Maybe that book you carry around

567
00:33:35.537 --> 00:33:38.637
makes you feel better about your situation,

568
00:33:38.638 --> 00:33:40.936
but I hope for your sake
that you start learning

569
00:33:40.937 --> 00:33:44.137
how to deal with your
problems in a real way,

570
00:33:44.138 --> 00:33:48.539
instead of hiding inside
of this fairy tale.

571
00:33:49.038 --> 00:33:53.106
- Uh, I've read fairy tales.

572
00:33:55.507 --> 00:33:57.006
Not a fairy tale.

573
00:33:57.007 --> 00:33:59.440
- Invisible creatures
in the air all around us

574
00:33:59.441 --> 00:34:02.007
fighting for our lost souls?

575
00:34:02.008 --> 00:34:05.074
And the only way to be
redeemed is by a blood sacrifice

576
00:34:05.075 --> 00:34:09.576
of a God come to Earth
and born of a virgin?

577
00:34:12.810 --> 00:34:15.110
That's some fairy tale, son.

578
00:34:17.110 --> 00:34:20.910
- I came in here to make you feel better.

579
00:34:20.911 --> 00:34:23.211
Looks like I did.

580
00:34:25.178 --> 00:34:28.746
It was nice meeting you,
you have a good night.

581
00:34:39.581 --> 00:34:42.281
- What if you're
wrong and God is real?

582
00:34:42.314 --> 00:34:44.047
How are you going to explain yourself

583
00:34:44.048 --> 00:34:45.482
to the Creator of the universe

584
00:34:45.515 --> 00:34:47.047
when all you've done is fight him?

585
00:34:47.048 --> 00:34:48.281
- I've tried to get a statue removed

586
00:34:48.282 --> 00:34:50.649
from the front of a courthouse, big deal.

587
00:34:50.650 --> 00:34:53.515
If anything, God would
need to explain Himself.

588
00:34:53.516 --> 00:34:55.248
- Like what?

589
00:34:55.249 --> 00:34:57.216
- Brain cancer in children?

590
00:34:57.217 --> 00:34:58.916
Why would he allow that?

591
00:34:58.917 --> 00:35:01.183
Babies born without their skulls?

592
00:35:01.184 --> 00:35:03.551
Why would a supreme being create a world

593
00:35:03.552 --> 00:35:07.150
that is filled with misery and pain

594
00:35:07.151 --> 00:35:09.251
and then try to blame it on us?

595
00:35:18.620 --> 00:35:20.087
And then I'm supposed to fall on my knees

596
00:35:20.088 --> 00:35:23.153
and worship this, this monster?

597
00:35:23.154 --> 00:35:27.655
No, if God is real, then
He's clearly a sadistic,

598
00:35:27.656 --> 00:35:30.823
blood-thirsty, evil
being, and doesn't deserve

599
00:35:30.855 --> 00:35:33.557
even the slightest praise from our lips.

600
00:36:41.601 --> 00:36:44.235
- I don't understand.

601
00:36:55.937 --> 00:36:58.969
- Hey, man, it's
James, I just wanted to call

602
00:36:58.970 --> 00:37:01.571
and let you know that mom, dad, and I

603
00:37:01.572 --> 00:37:03.170
are not going to be
able to make it to your

604
00:37:03.171 --> 00:37:05.471
away game tonight, um,

605
00:37:05.472 --> 00:37:07.472
Michael's not doing too good,

606
00:37:07.473 --> 00:37:09.705
so we're gonna head over there,

607
00:37:09.706 --> 00:37:13.306
but, uh, anyway, good luck, man.

608
00:37:13.307 --> 00:37:15.467
I know you're gonna kick
some Bison butt tonight, so...

609
00:37:41.344 --> 00:37:43.176
- Here we
go, Spartans, here we go.

610
00:37:43.177 --> 00:37:44.711
- Spartan left, 34 ISO.

611
00:37:44.712 --> 00:37:46.177
- Spartan left, 34 ISO. One to one.

612
00:37:46.178 --> 00:37:47.579
Ready!

613
00:37:52.012 --> 00:37:54.412
- Here we
go, Spartans, here we go.

614
00:38:09.683 --> 00:38:10.815
- Yeah.

615
00:38:10.816 --> 00:38:12.750
Time out, time out, time out.

616
00:38:46.589 --> 00:38:47.621
- Here's what we're gonna do,

617
00:38:47.622 --> 00:38:50.122
we're gonna go for two,
we've gotta punch this in.

618
00:38:50.123 --> 00:38:52.722
Win this in overtime, alright?

619
00:38:52.723 --> 00:38:55.323
Gun left, 17 ISO.

620
00:38:55.324 --> 00:38:57.189
Gotta run your tail off, bud.

621
00:38:57.190 --> 00:38:59.022
You have got to block hard, alright?

622
00:38:59.023 --> 00:39:00.922
It's the only way it's gonna work.

623
00:39:00.923 --> 00:39:02.257
Let's win this thing.

624
00:39:02.258 --> 00:39:03.657
Get in here, Spartans on three.

625
00:39:03.658 --> 00:39:05.657
One, two, three.
- Spartans!

626
00:41:27.215 --> 00:41:29.281
- Hello there.

627
00:41:29.282 --> 00:41:32.449
Hey, you, uh, barged into my room,

628
00:41:32.450 --> 00:41:35.016
so I thought I'd come
and barge into yours.

629
00:41:36.717 --> 00:41:38.750
- What can I do for you?

630
00:41:38.751 --> 00:41:40.383
- Look, I brought you
some reading material,

631
00:41:40.384 --> 00:41:44.084
since you sit here all day
with your nose in that fairy tale.

632
00:41:44.085 --> 00:41:45.851
These are awesome books.

633
00:41:45.852 --> 00:41:48.751
Stephen Hawking, "A
Brief History of Time",

634
00:41:48.752 --> 00:41:51.685
Richard Dawkins, "The Magic of Reality",

635
00:41:51.686 --> 00:41:53.353
and, of course, my favorite

636
00:41:53.386 --> 00:41:55.787
is Carl Sagan, "Cosmos".

637
00:41:55.788 --> 00:41:57.353
- I've read 'em.

638
00:41:57.354 --> 00:41:59.820
- You've read 'em and you
still read that garbage?

639
00:41:59.821 --> 00:42:02.855
- Interesting theories by brilliant men.

640
00:42:04.455 --> 00:42:07.522
Obviously, I don't agree with everything,

641
00:42:07.523 --> 00:42:09.489
but when I read those books,

642
00:42:10.989 --> 00:42:13.756
it reminds me what a great
creator God really is.

643
00:42:13.757 --> 00:42:16.055
Dude.

644
00:42:16.056 --> 00:42:17.589
- Listen, right now is not the best time

645
00:42:17.590 --> 00:42:20.323
for a theological debate.

646
00:42:20.324 --> 00:42:22.825
Maybe we can try another day.

647
00:42:24.591 --> 00:42:27.892
- 'Cause the little girl died.

648
00:42:27.893 --> 00:42:29.659
I'm sorry.

649
00:42:31.260 --> 00:42:34.058
I know she meant a lot to you.

650
00:42:34.059 --> 00:42:37.193
But she is in heaven, right?

651
00:42:37.194 --> 00:42:39.828
I mean, that's what you believe.

652
00:42:40.895 --> 00:42:42.126
- Yep.

653
00:42:45.928 --> 00:42:48.495
Doesn't mean I understand it.

654
00:42:49.530 --> 00:42:53.796
- It hurts when a fairy
tale starts to crumble away.

655
00:42:53.797 --> 00:42:57.430
- You may not have the same beliefs,

656
00:42:57.431 --> 00:42:59.263
and you might think I'm a wishful thinker,

657
00:42:59.264 --> 00:43:01.798
and that I'm naive,

658
00:43:01.799 --> 00:43:04.831
but you tell me what's harder to believe,

659
00:43:04.832 --> 00:43:07.765
that our finely-tuned
universe that we live in,

660
00:43:07.766 --> 00:43:10.064
full of life, means nothing?

661
00:43:10.065 --> 00:43:12.532
Or that God is given it
more purpose than you

662
00:43:12.533 --> 00:43:15.734
and your books can even
begin to understand?

663
00:43:18.133 --> 00:43:20.668
He's the most brilliant mind.

664
00:43:22.034 --> 00:43:24.435
- We don't have to talk.

665
00:43:25.602 --> 00:43:27.736
Can we just play dice?

666
00:43:29.536 --> 00:43:32.068
- You play Yahtzee?

667
00:43:32.069 --> 00:43:34.603
- I'll kick your butt.

668
00:43:41.504 --> 00:43:43.772
- Yahtzee.

669
00:43:45.005 --> 00:43:46.204
- Impossible.

670
00:43:46.205 --> 00:43:47.371
- It's not impossible.

671
00:43:47.372 --> 00:43:48.704
- No, you're changing out the dice.

672
00:43:48.705 --> 00:43:49.906
- I swear to you, I'm not.

673
00:43:49.907 --> 00:43:53.206
- Oh, I doubt you swear.

674
00:43:54.106 --> 00:43:56.239
- Maybe, it's God telling
you that I'm right.

675
00:43:56.240 --> 00:43:57.873
- Ha!

676
00:43:57.874 --> 00:44:02.574
- So, how long have you
been a non-believer?

677
00:44:02.575 --> 00:44:04.240
- A free-thinker, you mean?

678
00:44:04.241 --> 00:44:06.674
- Okay, how long have
you been a free-thinker?

679
00:44:06.675 --> 00:44:09.743
- Believe it or not, I
was once naive like you.

680
00:44:10.543 --> 00:44:11.743
- So what changed ya?

681
00:44:14.943 --> 00:44:16.743
- If I had to pinpoint it on one thing,

682
00:44:16.744 --> 00:44:19.510
I'd say it was the war.

683
00:44:19.511 --> 00:44:22.812
I came home and my marriage
quickly fell apart.

684
00:44:24.044 --> 00:44:26.479
I bought a cabin by the lake,

685
00:44:27.579 --> 00:44:30.812
took up carving, I tried
to forget those images

686
00:44:30.813 --> 00:44:33.146
that were burned in my brain.

687
00:44:34.814 --> 00:44:38.146
I used to think God was
this all-powerful being

688
00:44:38.147 --> 00:44:42.916
who looked after us,
but now I know better.

689
00:44:44.081 --> 00:44:47.515
The only one who's looking
after you, Michael, is you.

690
00:44:49.917 --> 00:44:52.182
- It's a pretty sad outlook on life.

691
00:44:52.183 --> 00:44:55.283
- It's just the truth.

692
00:44:55.284 --> 00:44:56.650
- Not for me, it isn't.

693
00:44:56.651 --> 00:44:57.917
- Oh, yeah?

694
00:44:57.918 --> 00:44:59.883
And what makes you so special?

695
00:44:59.884 --> 00:45:01.818
- I'm not saying I'm special.

696
00:45:01.819 --> 00:45:05.851
My life is no more or less
amazing than anybody else's,

697
00:45:05.852 --> 00:45:10.853
but everywhere I look, I
can see God's handiwork.

698
00:45:11.519 --> 00:45:12.952
In everything.

699
00:45:12.953 --> 00:45:14.886
- You know what they call that, Michael?

700
00:45:14.887 --> 00:45:18.553
They call that "confirmation bias."

701
00:45:18.554 --> 00:45:20.454
You see anything that you want to see,

702
00:45:20.455 --> 00:45:22.654
and when it does not
fit in your little box,

703
00:45:22.655 --> 00:45:24.487
you reject it.

704
00:45:24.488 --> 00:45:25.788
- I know what you're gettin' at, okay?

705
00:45:25.789 --> 00:45:28.721
Yes, humans are pattern-seekers
who see shapes in clouds

706
00:45:28.722 --> 00:45:31.189
and Jesus' face in potato chips.

707
00:45:31.190 --> 00:45:35.122
That's fine, but,

708
00:45:35.123 --> 00:45:37.323
haven't you ever had something

709
00:45:37.324 --> 00:45:40.623
happen to you where you knew that it had

710
00:45:40.624 --> 00:45:41.890
nothing to do with chance.

711
00:45:41.891 --> 00:45:44.758
That it had something to do with

712
00:45:44.759 --> 00:45:47.559
something greater than you?

713
00:45:48.725 --> 00:45:50.859
- In the world of random chance,

714
00:45:50.860 --> 00:45:52.892
things are bound to happen.

715
00:45:52.893 --> 00:45:54.358
- So nothing then?

716
00:45:54.359 --> 00:45:55.626
- Nothing what?

717
00:45:55.627 --> 00:45:57.561
- Nothing greater than you?

718
00:46:00.060 --> 00:46:01.861
- When we get out of this place,

719
00:46:01.862 --> 00:46:04.427
you're comin' with me up to my cabin,

720
00:46:04.428 --> 00:46:05.727
and after we catch our dinner,

721
00:46:05.728 --> 00:46:08.295
we're gonna carve away
on a couple of blocks

722
00:46:08.296 --> 00:46:13.062
as the sun sets on the
waters smooth as glass,

723
00:46:13.063 --> 00:46:16.062
and when the last rays of
sunlight hits your face,

724
00:46:16.063 --> 00:46:19.163
you're gonna get the
calls of a thousand voices

725
00:46:19.164 --> 00:46:22.697
that maybe you forgot shared

726
00:46:22.698 --> 00:46:26.097
this random, chaotic, painful,

727
00:46:26.098 --> 00:46:28.866
beautiful world with us.

728
00:46:32.066 --> 00:46:33.965
- Sounds good.

729
00:46:33.966 --> 00:46:35.632
- Then it's set then?

730
00:46:35.633 --> 00:46:36.633
- It's set.

731
00:46:36.634 --> 00:46:38.567
- You're gonna have to give
me a couple of weeks, though.

732
00:46:38.568 --> 00:46:41.266
I've been in this place too stinkin' long

733
00:46:41.267 --> 00:46:44.100
and who knows what it looks like now.

734
00:46:49.169 --> 00:46:51.202
- Gah, this
game does not agree with me.

735
00:46:51.203 --> 00:46:53.335
- No, it doesn't.

736
00:46:53.336 --> 00:46:55.470
- Can we pick up on it later?

737
00:46:55.471 --> 00:46:57.136
- Yeah.

738
00:46:57.137 --> 00:47:01.270
- Ya know, Michael,

739
00:47:01.271 --> 00:47:02.504
science does have a answer

740
00:47:02.505 --> 00:47:06.437
to how we live in such
a finely-tuned universe.

741
00:47:06.438 --> 00:47:08.205
You ever heard of quantum mechanics?

742
00:47:08.206 --> 00:47:09.538
- I've heard of it.
- Yeah.

743
00:47:09.539 --> 00:47:12.271
It predicts an infinite
number of universes

744
00:47:12.272 --> 00:47:14.940
with different properties in each one.

745
00:47:14.941 --> 00:47:17.841
Most are empty of life.

746
00:47:19.074 --> 00:47:21.006
This one works.

747
00:47:21.007 --> 00:47:23.607
So, maybe there's an alternate universe

748
00:47:23.608 --> 00:47:27.909
where you and I are playing
Yahtzee cancer-free.

749
00:47:27.943 --> 00:47:31.076
- And I whip your butt every game.

750
00:47:36.076 --> 00:47:38.210
- An infinite number of universes

751
00:47:38.211 --> 00:47:41.443
with an infinite number of possibilities

752
00:47:41.444 --> 00:47:44.512
and you're saying there's no room for God?

753
00:47:48.947 --> 00:47:52.580
- There is no miracle, son, I'm sorry.

754
00:47:53.880 --> 00:47:56.513
I wish I was wrong about that,

755
00:47:56.514 --> 00:47:58.580
but I'm not.

756
00:48:21.451 --> 00:48:22.451
- Hey, guys.

757
00:48:22.452 --> 00:48:24.016
- Hey, Dr. McAlpine.

758
00:48:24.017 --> 00:48:25.918
- Well, I thought I was comin' in here

759
00:48:25.919 --> 00:48:28.218
today to give you guys some bad news,

760
00:48:28.219 --> 00:48:29.585
to tell you that the second round

761
00:48:29.586 --> 00:48:31.386
of chemotherapy had failed,

762
00:48:31.420 --> 00:48:34.386
but, actually, it is pretty remarkable.

763
00:48:34.420 --> 00:48:37.086
I've never seen anything
like this in my career.

764
00:48:37.087 --> 00:48:39.854
Yesterday, your blast cells were at 80%

765
00:48:39.855 --> 00:48:42.086
and today, this morning's tests,

766
00:48:42.087 --> 00:48:44.222
and I double-checked these tests.

767
00:48:45.988 --> 00:48:48.322
You're in complete remission.

768
00:48:49.556 --> 00:48:52.388
Now, there's a pretty
good chance of relapse,

769
00:48:52.389 --> 00:48:56.256
so we need to start planning
for a bone marrow transplant.

770
00:48:56.257 --> 00:48:57.890
That planning means we need to find

771
00:48:57.891 --> 00:49:00.657
the best possible match for Michael,

772
00:49:00.658 --> 00:49:05.558
but for right now, get outta here.

773
00:49:06.625 --> 00:49:09.024
You can go home.

774
00:50:17.704 --> 00:50:21.504
- I cannot wait to go on
a proper date with you.

775
00:50:21.505 --> 00:50:23.203
We can go to the movie theater

776
00:50:23.204 --> 00:50:26.004
and we can buy one of those
really big buckets of popcorn

777
00:50:26.005 --> 00:50:29.204
and just drench it in butter,
and buy all the candy...

778
00:50:29.205 --> 00:50:30.873
- That's such a good idea,

779
00:50:30.874 --> 00:50:32.906
because nothing says, "I just beat cancer"

780
00:50:32.907 --> 00:50:34.539
than going into cardiac arrest.

781
00:50:34.540 --> 00:50:37.974
- Hey, you can bring Sheila,
we could make it a double-date.

782
00:50:37.975 --> 00:50:40.875
- No, no, no, I don't
wanna intrude, you guys go.

783
00:50:42.441 --> 00:50:44.208
- What's wrong?

784
00:50:45.442 --> 00:50:48.810
- Do you guys wanna do
something really cool?

785
00:51:15.146 --> 00:51:17.145
- Hi, I'm Michelle.
- Tony,

786
00:51:17.146 --> 00:51:19.146
it's nice to meet you.

787
00:51:19.147 --> 00:51:20.514
- Michael.
- Yeah, hi.

788
00:51:20.515 --> 00:51:22.113
- Nice to meet you.
